 {"id":"DF-SPECLANG-9","status":"complete","priority":"P3","type":"bug","complexity":2,"title":"P3 — validate --format json drops the failing spec from reports and prefixes a banner: a machine consumer sees 451/451 clean while the run exited 1","reasoning":"Dogfood 2026-11-16 (tick speclang-dogfood-2026-11-16-05-02-33, rev 08b77a59): the human path and the machine path disagree about whether the suite is clean. Bare `./bin/speclang validate` exits 1 and prints one parse error to stderr; `--format json` on the same tree returns reports.length=451 while the tree holds 452 spec files (450 *.spec.md + 2 *.scl), and NO report entry exists for the failed file, so `.reports[]|select((.errors|length)>0)` is empty. A CI step or agent that reads the JSON form (the reason to ask for JSON) concludes the suite is green. Separately, stdout line 1 is a human banner, so `validate --format json | jq` fails outright and consumers must know to strip line 1 — the failure the JSON mode exists to avoid. Fix direction: in the per-file catch, push a report object carrying the parse error before continuing, and route the banner to stderr (or suppress it) in json mode.","acceptance_criteria":"AC1 `./bin/speclang validate --format json` emits reports whose length equals the spec file count (452: 450 *.spec.md + 2 *.scl) and at least one report carries the NASA playbook parse error, so `.reports[]|select((.errors|length)>0)` is non-empty. AC2 the same invocation parses as JSON from stdout without stripping a leading banner line. AC3 `./bin/speclang validate` still exits 1 and still prints the per-file error to stderr, and the passing files report unchanged.","detail":{"metadata":{},"fingerprint":"22825016c7a5a69b3ea9eed6793b357f9c25436bed921eaddd92c88d6ff68bad","evidence":[{"run_id":"b981a312","ts":"2026-11-16T16:22:39.103776+00:00"}]},"capability_tags":["cli","validation","json-output","observability"],"created_at":"2026-11-16T06:52:45.513838+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-16T16:22:39.103776+00